{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U2"],
      "pins": ["U2.1"],
      "nets": ["+3.3V"],
      "observed": "PCA9685PW address-select pin A0 (pin 1) is tied to the +3.3V net, while A1 to A5 (pins 2, 3, 4, 5, 24) are on GND. With A0 high the device responds at 0x41, not 0x40.",
      "expected": "The specification lists the PWM driver at I2C address 0x40, which requires all address pins (A0 to A5) tied low, as the other address pins are.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"A0\") (pintype \"input\"))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A1\") (pintype \"input\"))"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "PWM/servo driver | FCCPeripherals | NXP PCA9685PW (I2C 16-ch PWM), I2C addr 0x40"}
      ],
      "impact": "The FCC will not find the servo driver at its expected address 0x40, so PWM output commands will fail or be sent to the wrong device.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["FCC1"],
      "pins": ["FCC1.73", "FCC1.48"],
      "nets": ["UART3_RX_FCC", "UART3_TX_FCC"],
      "observed": "The UART3 signal nets are connected to FCC1 pins 73 and 48, which the library defines as VCAP, a power_out pin for the internal core regulator decoupling. The UART3 connector pins are therefore driven by or shorted to the MCU's VCAP node.",
      "expected": "UART3 RX and TX should land on GPIO pins with a UART alternate function (for example the PB10/PB11 pins that are otherwise unused). They should not share a node with the VCAP regulator output.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"145\") (name \"UART3_RX_FCC\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"FCC1\") (pin \"73\") (pinfunction \"VCAP\") (pintype \"power_out\"))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"FCC1\") (pin \"48\") (pinfunction \"VCAP\") (pintype \"power_out\")) (node (ref \"UART3_FCC1\") (pin \"3\")"}
      ],
      "impact": "The core regulator output is loaded by the UART connector, and the UART cannot work. This can also damage the MCU's internal regulator.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_rating",
      "components": ["C1", "C2"],
      "pins": ["C1.1", "C2.1"],
      "nets": ["REG_VIN"],
      "observed": "The input bulk capacitors have no voltage rating field. C1 is a 100u part in a 6.3x7.7 electrolytic footprint, which is typically a low-voltage (6.3 to 16 V) package. C2 is a 47u part in a 1210 footprint.",
      "expected": "The specification says the input bulk capacitors must be rated at 35 V or more to cover the 26 V maximum battery input.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "input bulk capacitors \"C1, C2 rated at >= 35V\" (headroom for the 26 V max input)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"C1\") (value \"100u\") (footprint \"Capacitor_SMD:C_Elec_6.3x7.7\")"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the parts are rated below the battery voltage, the input capacitors may fail under a 26 V supply or transient.",
      "confidence": 0.15
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The most concrete deviation is the PCA9685 address pin A0 tied high, which moves the servo driver away from the specified 0x40 address. The UART3 nets on the FCC VCAP pins and the input capacitor ratings are weaker candidates."
}

